{
  "id": "gqarr-020",
  "title": "Reverse, now with reverse()",
  "concept": "gq-arrays",
  "difficulty": 1,
  "prompt": "Same task, this time with the built-in reverse method. [1, 2, 3] becomes [3, 2, 1].",
  "signature": "func reverse_with_method(items):",
  "fn": "reverse_with_method",
  "starter": "func reverse_with_method(items):\n\treturn items",
  "tests": [
    {
      "name": "[1, 2, 3] becomes [3, 2, 1]",
      "args": [
        [
          1,
          2,
          3
        ]
      ],
      "expect": [
        3,
        2,
        1
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Empty stays empty",
      "args": [
        []
      ],
      "expect": []
    },
    {
      "name": "One item stays",
      "args": [
        [
          7
        ]
      ],
      "expect": [
        7
      ]
    }
  ],
  "hints": [
    "reverse() changes the array in place and gives nothing back, so `return items.reverse()` will not even compile. Call it on its own line, then return the array.",
    "`items.reverse()` then `return items`."
  ],
  "docs": [
    {
      "name": "list.reverse()",
      "what": "Reverses the array in place. Returns nothing."
    }
  ],
  "solution": "func reverse_with_method(items):\n\titems.reverse()\n\treturn items",
  "generator": [
    {
      "array": {
        "len": [
          0,
          5
        ],
        "of": {
          "int": [
            1,
            10
          ]
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
